SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 311 | Friday, March 13, 2026

Good morning. Friday the 13th, and the freight market is behaving accordingly: calmly.

1) Transpacific spot flat for the first week since January
Shanghai to LA held at $1,890 per FEU. The blank sailings finally matched demand. Carriers are announcing a $300 general rate increase for April 1 that nobody expects to stick in full; half of it might.

2) Panama Canal auctions extra transit slots for April
The authority will auction eight additional daily Neopanamax slots through April, a sign that water levels are comfortable. Slot prices in the last auction averaged $310,000. LNG carriers took most of them.

3) An autonomous truck operator wins a middle-mile pilot with a national parcel carrier
Dallas to Houston, 240 miles, no safety driver, starting in May. The parcel carrier's own drivers will do the first and last miles. The operator's stock rose 22%. We have seen four of these pilots announced and one become a contract; the odds are what they are.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: $1.6B. Venture funding into supply chain software in Q1 so far, per a tracker out this morning, on pace for the weakest quarter since 2019. The money that is flowing is going to AI-flavored planning and to robotics; everything else is quiet.

Have a good weekend.

